Every time we open a website, the browser and the web server exchange data according to a set of rules. These rules are called HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol). 
The protocol was created by Tim Berners-Lee and his team between 1989 and 1991. HTTP describes how a client requests web page content (HTML, images, videos and so on) from a server and how the server delivers it. 
HTTP has a secure version, HTTPS, in which data is encrypted. 
Encryption solves two problems: outsiders cannot read what we send and receive, and we can be sure we are talking to the real server and not to someone impersonating it.

Before requesting anything, the browser needs to know where to go and how to get there. 
This is what a URL (Uniform Resource Locator) is for: essentially an instruction for accessing a resource. 
A URL consists of several parts, although not all of them are used in every request. The scheme specifies the protocol: HTTP, HTTPS, FTP. 
The user part lets you pass a username and password if the service requires authentication. The host is the domain name or IP address of the server. 
The port defines which port to connect to: usually 80 for HTTP and 443 for HTTPS, but any port from 1 to 65535 is allowed. The path points to the file or resource. 
The query string passes extra parameter for example:
/blog?id=1 requests the article with ID 1. 
Finally, the fragment refers to a specific location on the page, so the user immediately sees the right section of a long text.

The request itself can be very simple. Technically, one line is enough: GET / HTTP/1.1. In practice, the browser adds headers containing additional information:

http
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: tryhackme.com
User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 Firefox/87.0
Referer: https://tryhackme.com/

The first line specifies the GET method, the requested home page / and protocol version 1.1. The following lines tell the server which website is wanted, which browser the client is using and which page referred them. 
A request always ends with a blank line, which tells the server that the request is complete.

The server’s response is structured similarly. The first line contains the protocol version and a status code, such as 200 OK. 
Then come the headers: the server software (Server: nginx/1.15.8), the date and time, the content type (Content-Type) and its size (Content-Length), which lets the client confirm that no data is missing. 
After a blank line comes the content itself, for example the page’s HTML code.

The client expresses its intention through HTTP methods. Four are used most often: GET to retrieve information, POST to submit data and create new records, PUT to update existing data and DELETE to remove it. 
In everyday work, GET and POST are by far the most common.

The server reports the outcome of a request with a status code. Codes fall into five ranges: 
100–199 informational (rarely seen today)
200–299 success
300–399 redirection
400–499 client errors 
500–599 server errors which usually indicate a serious problem. 

The most common codes are:

200 OK: the request succeeded; 201 Created: a new resource was created, such as a user or a post.
301 Moved Permanently: the page has moved for good; 302 Found: a temporary redirect.
400 Bad Request: something in the request is wrong or missing, such as a required parameter.
401 Not Authorised: you need to log in; 403 Forbidden: no access at all, even after logging in.
404 Not Found: the resource does not exist; 405 Method Not Allowed: the wrong method was used, for example GET instead of the expected POST.
500 Internal Server Error: the server could not process the request; 503 Service Unavailable: the server is overloaded or down for maintenance.

Headers deserve a closer look. Formally, none of them is required, but without them a website is hard to display properly. The most important client request headers are: 
Host (one server may host several websites, and this header names the one you want; otherwise you get the default site)
User-Agent (the browser and its version, so the server can format the page correctly)
Content-Length (how much data is being sent, for example from a form)
Accept-Encoding (the compression methods the browser supports) 
Cookie 

In response  the server sends 

Set-Cookie (data the browser will send back with every request)
Cache-Control (how long to keep the response in the cache)
Content-Type (the data type, which tells the browser how to process it) 
Content-Encoding (how the data was compressed).

Finally, cookies. These are small pieces of data stored on the user’s computer after receiving a Set-Cookie header and then sent back to the server with every new request. 
They are needed because HTTP is stateless: the server does not remember previous requests. Cookies remind it who we are, what our settings are and whether we have visited the site before. 
They are most commonly used for authentication, and a cookie usually holds not a plaintext password but a token, a unique secret code that is hard to guess. 
You can view your cookies in the browser’s developer tools: open the Network tab, select the request and open the Cookies tab.

